      },
      "targets": [
        {
          "refId": "A",
          "format": "time_series",
          "rawSql": "SELECT trading_date::timestamptz AS \"time\", close FROM silver.prices_daily WHERE ticker = '^VIX' ORDER BY 1"
        }
      ],
      "options": {
        "reduceOptions": {
          "calcs": [
            "lastNotNull"
          ]
        },
        "graphMode": "area",
        "colorMode": "value"
      },
      "fieldConfig": {
        "defaults": {
          "decimals": 1,
          "thresholds": {
            "mode": "absolute",
            "steps": [
              {
                "color": "green",
                "value": null
              },
              {
                "color": "yellow",
                "value": 20
              },
              {
                "color": "red",
                "value": 30
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        "overrides": []
      },
      "description": "The market's 'fear gauge': the volatility the options market EXPECTS in the S&P 500 over the next 30 days. It is a forecast of turbulence, not a direction — it rises in crashes because violent moves are expected, not because prices must fall.\n\nRough guide: under 15 = calm · 15-20 = normal · 20-30 = nervous · above 30 = fear · above 50 = panic (seen in 2008 and March 2020).\n\nWHY IT MATTERS HERE: fear is what widens CEF discounts. When the VIX spikes, retail holders sell closed-end funds faster than the underlying bonds move, so price falls further than NAV and the discount opens — which is exactly the anomaly this dashboard hunts. A calm VIX with wide discounts is a different (and rarer) signal than a panicked VIX with wide discounts.\n\nIt mean-reverts hard: spikes are violent and short-lived, so a high reading is a moment to look, not a level that lasts."
    },
    {
      "id": 22,
      "type": "timeseries",
      "title": "Is today's spread normal? - long-run context (Baa proxy for the panel above)",
      "description": "The context the panel above cannot give. Moody's Baa corporate bond yield minus the 10-year Treasury: the same idea as the HY spread (what investors demand for taking credit risk) but on investment-grade bonds — and, crucially, NOT licence-restricted, so it reaches back to 2007 instead of 3 years.\n\nThe two are not interchangeable: Baa is higher quality, so its LEVELS are lower than high yield. What transfers is the SHAPE — where we sit relative to calm and to crisis.\n\nThe flat line is today's level, drawn across the whole window so you can see at a glance whether now is historically calm or stressed. For reference, this spread peaked at 6.16% in the 2008 crisis; today it is around 1.6%.\n\nUse the time picker (top right) to widen or narrow the window — zoom out to include 2008 and 2020 for the full picture.",
      "datasource": {
        "type": "grafana-postgresql-datasource",
        "uid": "neon-sentinel"
      },
      "gridPos": {
        "h": 9,
        "w": 24,
