    {
      "id": 12,
      "type": "text",
      "title": "Column guide - CEF universe table",
      "gridPos": {
        "h": 8,
        "w": 24,
        "x": 0,
        "y": 29
      },
      "options": {
        "mode": "markdown",
        "content": "### Reading the CEF universe table\n\n| Column | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| **CEF** | Fund name and ticker. The universe is config-driven (tickers.yaml), not hardcoded. |\n| **Price** | Latest market price per share - unadjusted daily close (the real on-screen price). |\n| **NAV** | Net Asset Value per share published by the fund (via CEFConnect): what the portfolio is actually worth. |\n| **NAV date** | Date of the NAV shown. Published once a day after the close, so it normally lags price by one trading day. |\n| **NAV quality** | Freshness computed in SQL: *estimado* = up to 4 calendar days old (normal - weekends/holidays); *rancio* = older, the NAV scraper is likely failing. |\n| **Prem/Disc %** | (Price - NAV) / NAV. Negative = discount (you pay less than the portfolio is worth), positive = premium. Gradient over +/-15%. |\n| **Status** | The sign of Prem/Disc % as a word: Premium / Discount / Par. Some funds (e.g. PIMCO) habitually trade at persistent premiums. |\n| **Yield %** | Distribution yield on PRICE, trailing 12 months: everything paid in the last 12m divided by today's price. Deliberately NOT colored: a very high yield can signal distress, not a gift - see the yield table below for the NAV yield and the cut signal. |\n| **Payments/yr** | Distributions paid in the trailing 12 months (~12 = monthly payer, ~4 = quarterly). |\n| **Z-score** | How unusual today's premium/discount is versus this fund's OWN last year, in standard deviations. 0 = its normal level; -2 = unusually cheap (roughly the cheapest ~2% of days); +2 = unusually expensive. Big negative z-score with enough observations = the anomaly worth investigating. |\n| **Obs (1y)** | Daily observations behind the z-score (max ~252 trading sessions). Low count = young series - read the z-score with caution. |\n| **Z-score date** | Date of the last daily observation used in the z-score (updates after each NAV ingest). |"
      },
      "transparent": false
    },
    {
      "id": 11,
      "type": "table",
      "title": "Yield & distributions (TTM) - sorted by yield on price",
      "description": "Distribution yield per CEF from gold.yield_ttm. 'Yield on price' = what a buyer today collects (12 months of distributions / current price). 'Yield on NAV' = what the portfolio must actually earn to sustain the payout - the gap between the two IS the discount. 'Yield current' = last distribution annualized at the fund's own payment cadence; below the TTM yield means the fund recently CUT its distribution - the event that historically precedes discount blowouts. 'Dist trend' compares the current annualized rate vs what was actually paid over 12 months: negative = cutting, ~0 = stable. High yield is NOT automatically good: it can signal distress - read it together with the discount z-score above.",
      "gridPos": {
        "h": 10,
        "w": 24,
        "x": 0,
        "y": 37
      },
      "datasource": {
        "type": "grafana-postgresql-datasource",
        "uid": "neon-sentinel"
      },
      "targets": [
        {
          "refId": "A",
          "format": "table",
          "rawSql": "SELECT ticker AS \"CEF\", round(yield_ttm_on_price_pct, 2) AS \"Yield on price %\", round(yield_ttm_on_nav_pct, 2) AS \"Yield on NAV %\", round(yield_current_pct, 2) AS \"Yield current %\", round((yield_current_pct / yield_ttm_on_price_pct - 1) * 100, 1) AS \"Dist trend %\", dist_ttm AS \"Paid 12m $\", n_dist_ttm AS \"Payments (1y)\", last_amount AS \"Last dist $\", last_ex_date::text AS \"Last ex-date\" FROM gold.yield_ttm ORDER BY yield_ttm_on_price_pct DESC"
