    {
      "id": 21,
      "type": "text",
      "title": "How to read the backtest (honest caveats)",
      "gridPos": {
        "h": 8,
        "w": 12,
        "x": 12,
        "y": 56
      },
      "options": {
        "mode": "markdown",
        "content": "**Thesis under test:** when a CEF's discount becomes anomalously wide (z-score crosses below **-2**), does the discount *revert* (narrow) and the *price* rise over the next 1 / 3 / 6 months?\n\n- **Discount reverted %** / **Price up %** = share of *matured* signals where it happened.\n- **Sampling caveat:** so far ~half the signals come from the **2026-03 credit sell-off**, where nearly the whole universe crossed -2 at once and rebounded together. Those are **correlated**, not independent - a high hit rate today reflects *one episode*, not a proven edge.\n- **+6m has not matured yet** (needs 6 months of forward data). The sample grows with every accumulated trading day.\n- *Mechanical note:* a z-score is a deviation from a 1-year moving average, so some reversion is built in. **Price return** is the more independent signal."
      }
    }
